00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.980
Here is how it works when a group tries to choose by comparing markets that only pay if

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.160
a given option is picked.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.840
People first agree on a result they can measure.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.940
Traders then put a price on that result under each option, and the rule takes the option

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.600
whose conditional price looks better.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.020
A paper in frontiers in blockchain tested whether that design could run decentralized science.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:17.840
The authors looked at how 13 science DAOs govern themselves, then replayed old proposals

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.060
from Vita Dio, with a fixed rule rather than a coin flip, those passvotes and the market

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:27.900
preferred choice pointed the same way every time. They treat that match as a sign the two

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.680
systems could fit, and they say a live version still needs a clear indicator and traders who

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:38.440
do not all think alike. A decision market is the smaller piece. It asks people who want something

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:43.080
for themselves to reveal what they know, then builds a forecast that depends on the action.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:49.220
A 2023 paper on RXIV says the usual version buys honest answers with randomness.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:53.600
Now and then, the person in charge must take the step the forecast has already marked

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:54.080
as worse.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.380
The authors offer three ways to keep the choice fixed.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.920
If she already has a signal of her own, she scores the others against it and decides

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.160
from the pooled forecast plus that signal.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:09.440
If she has no signal, she can hand one person a cut of the profit and borrow that person's

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:09.720
signal.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.440
Or she can score two people against each other and use what they report.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:17.480
They tried the first version inside a learning system with many agents.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.600
Those agents reached a level close to a Bayesian model that could see every signal at once.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.440
So a conditional price is not a plain call on what happens next.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.220
It is a call on what happens if someone chooses.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:34.260
And it is only as usable as the score, the stand in and the indicator the group agreed

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:34.720
to chase.

